 { Below is an overview of concepts introduced in this chapter: (1) Open & semi-open files: While the majority of the course focuses on pawn and minor piece play, open/semi-open files are where the heavy pieces, notably the rooks, can really make their presence felt. In this chapter we explored the major themes and patterns of play along such files. (2) The battle for entry squares: The most famous entry point for a rook into the enemy position is the seventh rank. However, access to any of the ranks can prove significant. Usually entry on the fifth & sixth ranks offer some advantage, while entry along the seventh & eighth is in many instances decisive. (3) The value of outposts: Outposts are an important aspect of positional play generally. In the case of open or semi-open files they can be especially useful, since they provide structurally strong squares that the heavy pieces can use to support an invasion into enemy territory. (4) Semi-open files: Possessing a semi-open file is a considerable advantage, since it allows the attacking side to actively exert pressure with their rooks while the defending side is often forced to remain passive. The main themes we explored here were based around targeting backward pawns, as well as applying (and defending against) piece pressure. } 1. { -KEY- }  
