 { Black is threatening the typical ...Nc4 jump. Can you find a way for White to add piece control to the c4 square? } 1. { -KEY- } Na5 $1 Nc4 { Now this idea is not effective. } 2. Nxc4 $1 { Correct! The knight exchange leaves White with a dominant position. } ( 2. Bxc4 $6 { Giving up the bishop pair is less convincing. } bxc4 3. Nd5 Bxd5 4. Qxd5 $14 { White still stands better, but it's not ideal that their knight, is for the time being, stuck on a5. } ) 
bxc4 ( 2... Bxc4 $2 3. Bxc4 bxc4 4. Nd5 $18 { is a dream situation for White, with a monster knight on d5. } ) 
3. Nd5 $1 Bxd5 4. Qxd5 c3 { In the game, Black went for the pawn break but White can keep things locked down. } 5. b3 $18 { Black's attack has run out of steam. White is winning due to the mix of advantages: pressure on the d-file, space on the kingside, bishop pair, and pawn targets on a6 and c3. } 
