 { Find the winning sequence for White. } 35. { -KEY- } Rxc8 $1 { Black's knight and king are overloaded, so the exchange on c8 forces Black to surrender the g-file. } Rxc8 ( 35... Rxg1 { If Black plays this move, White can punish him using tactics: } 36. fxe6 fxe6 37. Rc5 Rg4 ( 37... Rf1 { would also lose material to } 38. Ke2 { . } ) 
38. Nh5 $18 { Black cannot stop both threats of Nf6+ and Rxa5. } ) 
 ( { If } 35... Kxc8 { then } 36. Rxg8 Nxg8 37. fxe6 $18 { when White is a clean pawn up. } ) 
36. Rg7 $1 { Correct! White capitalises on this by entering along the seventh rank, when there is already no way to save the position due to the pressure against the f7 pawn. } Rc2 ( 36... Nxf5 { This was another option, but not enough to hold. } 37. Rxf7 Ne7 38. Ng6 Bd8 39. Nd2 $18 { Black is lost here, with no good way to meet the multiple threats of Nxb3 and Rh7. } ) 
37. Rxf7 exf5 38. Nxd5 Ke6 39. Rf6+ ( 39. Rxe7 { was also winning. } ) 
Kd7 ( { If } 39... Kxd5 { it's mate after } 40. Rd6 ) 
40. Rd6+ Ke8 41. Nxe7 Kxe7 42. Rxa6 Bd8 43. d5 $18 { White went on to win the game in Astaneh Lopez,A - Ludy Sousa,P (2017). } 
