 { White has a promising position, but incredibly there is only one winning sequence. Can you find it? Warning: this puzzle is very challenging! } 1. { -KEY- } Rb3 $1 { White's attention had been on the semi-open f-file, but it's this sneaky transfer of the rook to the open b-file that crushes Black's hopes to survive in the chaos. } ( 1. Raf1 $2 { At first glance this move seems crushing, but Black has one saving resource: } f5 $1 ( 1. Rb3 f6 { would lose to } 2. Rb3 Nxe5 3. Bxe5 fxe5 4. Rb7 $1 Kxd6 5. Qd3 $1 { Black is forced to play ...Qd5 as the king is trapped. } ) 
2. exf6 Bxf6 3. Bxf6 Nxf6 { The dust has settled, and White's attacking chances are largely gone. } 4. Nb5 Nd7 $11 { Black keeps everything together, the position is equal. } ) 
Rab8 2. Rxb8 Rxb8 { Black is banking on recovering the piece on b2 if White captures the knight. } 3. Qxg4 $1 Rxb2 4. Qh4+ $1 { The critical check. } Kd7 5. Rf1 $1 { Threatening mate on f7. } ( 5. Qxh7 $4 Rxd2 { and suddenly Black is winning. } ) 
f5 6. Qxh7 $1 { Correct! Black is lost as the bishop will fall with check. Well done if you solved the puzzle from beginning to end, it's about as tough a puzzle as you'll find anywhere in the course. } 
